    <h2>The Downtown-Area Homes Are Running Out of Pipe</h2>

    <p>Walk the streets around the historic core off Main Street — the ones near the restored Freight House Restaurant — and most of the houses were plumbed in galvanized steel. Galvanized was the standard supply pipe into the 1960s, and it fails in a predictable way: the zinc coating gives out, the steel rusts from the inside, and a 3/4-inch line silts down until the water squeezes through a channel the width of a pencil. The homeowner notices it as pressure that has been quietly dying for years, then a faucet that trickles.</p>

    <p>The practitioner tell that separates a real plumber from a parts-changer: the hot side clogs before the cold, because heat accelerates the corrosion. If your shower runs weak hot and strong cold, the fixture is fine and the pipe is the problem. The other quiet failure point is the galvanized nipple threaded into the top of an old water heater — it rusts closed first and chokes the whole hot side of the house from one six-inch fitting. A plumber who has worked these homes checks that nipple before condemning anything expensive.</p>

    <h2>Cast Iron Drains and the Root Problem</h2>

    <p>The supply side is only half of it. The older homes drain through cast-iron sewer lines that scale and channel from the bottom up, and out at the street the lateral is often clay tile that tree roots have found. When every drain in the house slows down at once — not one sink, all of them — the trouble is at the main, and the only honest first step is a camera down the cleanout. Here is what goes wrong when a homeowner skips that step: a drain machine run blind through a cast-iron line that has rusted to a paper-thin channel can punch straight through the bottom of the pipe, turning a $250 snaking into a $4,800 excavation. The camera is $150 to $250 and it tells you whether you are dealing with a soft clog, hardened scale, or roots before anyone spins a blade.</p>

    <p>Honest limitation, because a good plumber will tell you this up front: snaking a root-filled clay lateral is a rent payment, not a cure. The roots come back on their own schedule, usually within a year or two, and the permanent answer is either a spot repair at the intrusion or a full liner. Anyone promising a one-time snake fixes roots forever is selling the callback.</p>

    <h2>The Newer Subdivisions Are a Different Trade</h2>

    <p>Out in Hickory Heights and Heritage Heights, the housing stock is young enough that the supply is PEX and the drains are modern PVC. These homes almost never have the pipe-failure problems of the older side of town. What they need instead is routine: a water heater that has hit its 8-to-12-year window and should be replaced on the homeowner's schedule, a pressure-reducing valve that has drifted high, a hose bib that froze and split, a garbage disposal that finally quit. It is steady, lower-drama work, and the commuter families who bought these houses want a plumber who answers the phone and shows up when promised more than they want a specialist in 1950s cast iron.</p>

    <p>That split is worth understanding as a homeowner, because it changes who you should hire. A plumber whose whole business is repipes and sewer excavation may treat your subdivision water-heater swap as an afterthought, and a service-first outfit may be out of its depth on a full galvanized house near downtown. The right question to ask on the phone is simply what kind of work they do most.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Plumbing Work Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and the reason each one moves:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Service call / diagnostic</strong> — about $95 to $185 to show up and find the problem, often credited toward the repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Water heater replacement</strong> — roughly $1,400 to $2,600 for a standard 40- or 50-gallon tank, more if the old connections have to be brought up to code.</li>
      <li><strong>Main line camera + snake</strong> — about $250 to $500; the camera is worth every dollar before anyone runs a machine through old cast iron.</li>
      <li><strong>Whole-house PEX repipe</strong> — roughly $6,500 to $12,000 on a three-bath home, driven almost entirely by how much wall and slab has to be opened to reach the runs.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The repipe range is wide for an honest reason: nobody can quote it accurately from the driveway. It depends on where the lines run, whether the house is on a slab or a crawlspace, and what the crew finds once the drywall is open.</p>

    <h2>When to Call Instead of Wait</h2>

    <p>Two situations do not reward patience. A water heater weeping rust at its base is corroding through and will eventually let go all at once, so replace it before it picks the moment. And a supply line that has started leaking behind a wall on a galvanized house is the first of many — where one section has rusted through, the rest is close behind, and chasing pinholes one at a time costs more over two years than the repipe would have. Everything else can usually wait for a scheduled appointment instead of an emergency rate.</p>

